# The RFC-049 audits hash these trees byte for byte (frozen specifications, vendor
# snapshots, retrieved primary-source evidence).  Git's automatic end-of-line
# normalisation would rewrite those bytes on checkout and silently break every
# recorded digest, so the trees are stored exactly as they are on disk.
data/rfc049/** -text

# The cost-barrier CI evidence bundle is digest-pinned: the copy of the gitignored
# market-horizon input must arrive in a clean checkout with exactly the bytes the
# published explorer recorded for it, so git must not normalise them on the way in
# or out.  Same reason as the RFC-049 trees above, same treatment.
data/recovery/wp7/evidence/** -text

# The WP-8 input-authority bundle is digest-pinned for the same reason as the
# cost-barrier one: every external input the ACTIVE recovery lane needs must arrive
# in a clean checkout with exactly the bytes its published artefact recorded, so git
# must not normalise them on the way in or out.
data/recovery/wp8/evidence/** -text

# The WP-EDGE-04R proposal is the definition the scheduler consumes, and ``schtasks`` reads
# that form (the document declares UTF-16).  It is committed in the bytes it is applied with
# and pinned by digest in the record beside it, so git must not normalise it either.
reports/edge-recovery/WP-EDGE-04R/evidence/PROPOSED_FORWARD_TASK.xml -text

# The WP-EDGE-09 evidence is digest-pinned by the package itself: every gate,
# experiment, ledger and report row quotes the sha256 of the files it was derived
# from, and the panel of one second is the only copy of the capture that survives a
# clone (the raw segments under reports/ are not versioned).  Normalising these bytes
# on checkout would break the digests the verifier recomputes, so the tree is stored
# exactly as it is on disk.  Same reason as the trees above, same treatment.
reports/edge-recovery/WP-EDGE-09/** -text

# The WP-EDGE-12 proposal is the definition a preflight hashes and an operator would
# register, and ``schtasks`` reads that form (the document declares UTF-16): the digest
# published beside it in RUNNER_PREFLIGHT.json is a digest of these bytes, so normalising
# them on the way in or out would make the recorded hash name a document nobody has.
reports/edge-recovery/WP-EDGE-12/evidence/PROPOSED_DEFINITION.xml -text

# The prospective holdout slices are digest-pinned: the capture manifest records the
# sha256 of the bytes the collector wrote, and a checkout that rewrites their line
# endings (core.autocrlf=true on this host did exactly that) makes the digest name a
# file nobody has.  Same reason as the digest-pinned trees above, same treatment.
reports/economic-recovery/RFC-EDGE-01*/scratch/*.jsonl -text

# EXP-003M publishes digests of its own artifacts (SHA256SUMS.txt) and of the regenerable
# tape (DATASET_SHA256SUMS.txt).  A clean checkout with core.autocrlf=true rewrote those
# files to CRLF and the seal verified 0/18: the hash named bytes nobody had.  The tree is
# stored exactly as written so the manifest is a check and not a coincidence.
reports/ai-bots/EXP-003M-MAKER-FILL-REALITY-001/** -text
